# The in-game Field Manual: a paged book opened by the Field Manual item. Keys are relative to Mods.Levy.Guide.
# Every page is Pages.<Name>.Title and Pages.<Name>.Body. A body is 3 to 6 lines, a few short declarative sentences per line.
# The page order lives in Common/UI/GuidePages.cs; GuideCheck logs a [GuideCheck] line for every page at boot.

Title: Field Manual
Contents: Contents
Previous: Previous
Next: Next
PageOf: Page {0} of {1}
CloseHint: Close the manual. Esc also closes it.
PreviousHint: The previous page. The Left arrow key does the same.
NextHint: The next page. The Right arrow key does the same.
# Hover text for an empty slot in the Armory's storage grid.
StorageHint: Storage holds anything. Levies use weapons, armor, accessories, grappling hooks, ammo, potions and throwables.
DepositJunkHint: Moves every weapon, armor piece, accessory and ammo stack that is not favorited from your pack into storage.

Pages: {
	Welcome: {
		Title: How to use this book
		Body:
			'''
			This manual teaches how to raise, arm and command levies.
			Pick a page from the contents on the left, or use Previous and Next.
			The Left and Right arrow keys turn pages. Esc closes the book.
			Use the Field Manual again to reopen it. It is never used up.
			'''
	}

	Barracks: {
		Title: Barracks and Regulars
		Body:
			'''
			Craft a Barracks at a Work Bench and place it in your town.
			Town NPCs housed within 120 tiles belong to it.
			Right-click the Barracks to open its panel. Enlist a Levy, a Man-at-Arms or a Champion there. Each one is a Regular: a named soldier who follows you and fights beside you.
			Regulars report to the Barracks that enlisted them. A fallen Regular returns after 60 seconds.
			Build an Armory within 120 tiles so they can arm themselves.
			'''
	}

	CommandPoints: {
		Title: Command points
		Body:
			'''
			Command points limit how many Regulars you can field. A Levy costs 1 point, a Man-at-Arms 2 and a Champion 3.
			The default is 14 points at the start, 28 at the Wall of Flesh, 34 at Moon Lord and 40 after the last boss. Each boss you beat adds its share.
			The Barracks panel shows the points used and the points held by Regulars in reserve.
			Enlisting or promoting needs free points and coins. Every grade is open from the start.
			Change the counts with Setup or the mod config.
			'''
	}

	Grades: {
		Title: Grades and growth
		Body:
			'''
			A Champion's aim spread is 1 degree, a Man-at-Arms 3 and a Levy 6.
			Promote raises a Regular one grade for the difference in muster fee and points. Demote lowers one grade and refunds nothing.
			A Regular who lives through 3 boss fights can become a Veteran. A Veteran aims 40% truer and fears less. At most a quarter of your Regulars are Veterans.
			Feed Life Crystals, Life Fruit and Mana Crystals from the More page of a Regular's card. Life tops out at 500 and mana at 200.
			'''
	}

	BossLife: {
		Title: Boss life and your army
		Body:
			'''
			A boss gains life from the soldiers who fight it. Each soldier who damages the boss adds 2% of its base life per command point he costs, times his gear strength.
			Gear strength is 1 for gear of the boss's own stage, 2 for gear three tiers ahead and one half for gear three tiers behind. The weapon counts 60% and the armor and accessories 40%.
			The gain stops at a cap: 80% before Hardmode, rising to 400% late in the game.
			Boss life from army strength and Boss life gain cap are under Settings, Mod Configuration, Levy. A strength of 1 is normal and 0 turns the gain off.
			The fight report states the life a fight added.
			'''
	}

	Wages: {
		Title: Wages and treasury
		Body:
			'''
			A Regular costs a muster fee up front, worth 5 days of wage, and a wage at dawn. Both come from the Barracks treasury. Deposit coins on the Barracks panel.
			A Levy earns 10 silver a day at the start, a Man-at-Arms 3 times that and a Champion 7 times. Wages rise as the world progresses. Return to barracks halves a wage and Reserve removes it.
			Soldiers on Follow me or standing idle, with no enemy within 25 tiles, collect loose coins and items within 6 tiles and carry them home. Coins go to the treasury.
			A Regular unpaid for 2 days may desert: a 25% chance each dawn, then 50%. His kit returns to the Armory.
			With Wage mutiny on in the mod config, unpaid Regulars refuse orders, rob townsfolk and attack you until the treasury pays them.
			Hazard pay on the Barracks panel pays every Regular one day's wage, once a day. They fear 25% less until dawn.
			'''
	}

	Reserve: {
		Title: Reserve
		Body:
			'''
			Reserve takes a Regular off active duty. His body leaves the field, and he draws no wage and holds no command points.
			Name, grade, gear and veteran status are kept. A Regular in reserve rests to full health at dawn and takes no orders.
			Press Reserve on a Regular's card, or Stand down all on the Barracks panel for everyone. Up to 100 soldiers can wait in reserve.
			Recall brings a Regular back while you have the points. Remobilize all recalls everyone it can.
			A Regular in mutiny cannot be sent to reserve.
			'''
	}

	Militia: {
		Title: Militia
		Body:
			'''
			Hold the War Horn near a Barracks and left-click to call up your town's militia. Left-click again to stand them down. The P key does the same within 120 tiles.
			Militia arm from the Armory and fight around the settlement. A settlement needs at least two town NPCs to raise them.
			Each town NPC adds 9 manpower. Every militiaman who falls uses 1. Manpower grows by 1 for every 2 town NPCs at dawn.
			Up to 10 militia stand at once. Calling them up costs a small fee per head from the treasury.
			A Recruiting drive at the Barracks refills missing manpower for coins, once a day.
			Auto-muster calls the militia when an event or a boss fight starts and stands them down when it ends. Switch it on the Barracks panel. Turn off Auto-muster for boss fights under Settings, Mod Configuration, Levy to keep it to events.
			'''
	}

	Orders: {
		Title: Giving orders
		Body:
			'''
			Right-click the War Horn to open the orders radial. A click on the Command Radio opens it, and so does the Orders button when you talk to a soldier. Pick an order. Right-click or Esc closes it.
			Keys need no item: Y follow, U hold, I return, O hold fire, P mobilize, K rally and N army overview. Rebind them under Settings, Controls.
			By horn, radial or key an order reaches Regulars within 150 tiles. Farther Regulars report that they could not hear it.
			A Command Radio anywhere in your pack reaches every Regular in the world.
			The Barracks panel orders every Regular at once, or one at a time, from any distance.
			An order lasts until you give another. Regulars in reserve take none.
			'''
	}

	OrdersMove: {
		Title: Orders - follow, hold, return
		Body:
			'''
			Follow me: Regulars follow you in ranks and fight within 30 tiles of you. A Regular more than 60 tiles away and out of sight teleports to you.
			Hold here: Regulars stay where they stand. They fight what comes in reach and walk back to the spot.
			Hold the banners: Regulars walk to the nearest Holding Banner and hold there. Soldiers within 5 tiles of a banner gain 12 defense and take 20% less damage. With no banner it is Hold here.
			Return to barracks: Regulars walk home and wait there at half wage.
			If you fall while a boss you have beaten is near, Regulars on Follow me hold where you fell and keep fighting it. Otherwise they walk home until you respawn.
			Any other order cancels these. A Holding Banner is crafted at a Work Bench from 8 wood and 3 silk.
			'''
	}

	OrdersHunt: {
		Title: Orders - hunting
		Body:
			'''
			Search & destroy: Regulars roam outward from the settlement hunting enemies, up to 120 tiles from their anchor. A Regular more than 60 tiles from you and out of sight teleports to you.
			Free rein: Regulars act fully on their own. They roam up to 600 tiles from the settlement, follow the fighting and never teleport to you.
			Regroup & engage: Regulars hunt as in Search & destroy. In a boss fight those in the fight act as in Free rein, and they hunt again when it ends. A Regular who respawns appears beside you.
			Ranged soldiers keep their distance on all three. Any other order cancels them.
			'''
	}

	OrdersFire: {
		Title: Orders - fire, ranks and focus
		Body:
			'''
			Hold fire: soldiers, their summons and officers stop attacking. The button then reads Fire at will, and the O key toggles it.
			Spread out keeps wide gaps, even when idle, and blunts area attacks. Close up packs soldiers tight around you and keeps fights within 14 tiles of you. The one button cycles Spread out, Close up and Normal ranks.
			Focus attack: press it, then click an enemy, a critter or a spot. The squad attacks it until it is clear. Clear focus, a second click on the same target, or Esc while choosing cancels it.
			Re-arm: every soldier returns their kit to the Armory and draws again from the issue slots.
			Show names displays every soldier's nameplate. Press it again for the normal fade.
			'''
	}

	Rally: {
		Title: Rally
		Body:
			'''
			Rally steadies soldiers who are afraid. Press K, or pick Rally on the radial.
			Every Regular within 30 tiles of you settles, and a shaken or routed Regular goes back to its order. With a Command Radio it reaches every Regular in the world.
			It works once a minute. Pressing early says how many seconds remain.
			Fear builds while a boss is in view and when comrades fall. A shaken soldier aims worse and deals 40% less damage. A routed soldier backs away from the threat.
			A routed soldier also recovers after 20 seconds out of sight of a boss. Standing within 10 tiles halves how fast soldiers fear.
			Rally does not move soldiers. Follow me calls them to you.
			'''
	}

	ActiveArmy: {
		Title: Active army size
		Body:
			'''
			Active army size sets how many of your Regulars fight at once. The rest wait in reserve on full duty.
			Set it in the Barracks beside Stand down all, in Levy: Performance, or from the lag note.
			It is off by default, and 0 fields everyone. The suggested size is 30.
			When a soldier falls, the next one waiting takes his place right away.
			Fewer soldiers on the field means less lag but less firepower. Below about 30, the army can be overwhelmed.
			'''
	}

	BarracksOrders: {
		Title: Orders from the Barracks
		Body:
			'''
			The Barracks panel gives orders from any distance. All Regulars has Follow, Hold and Return, and each Regular's card has the same three.
			Mobilize and Stand down call and send home the militia. Stand down all and Remobilize all move every Regular to and from the reserve.
			A card also has Kit, Promote, Demote and Reserve. More holds Patrol, Rebase, Discharge and feeding.
			Patrol walks a Regular between the two nearest Patrol Banners. Place the banners within 60 tiles of the Barracks.
			Send to map point marches every Regular to a spot on the map. Reset loadouts returns every kit to the Armory for a fresh issue.
			'''
	}

	MapOrders: {
		Title: Map orders
		Body:
			'''
			Map orders send your Regulars to a point on the full map. They march there, fight what is in reach and hold the spot.
			They need A Guide to Autonomy, studied once in the world.
			Pick Send to map point on the orders radial or the Barracks panel. Double-click a spot on the full map. Dragging moves the map and never sends. Esc cancels.
			With a Command Radio in your pack, hold Shift and double-click anywhere on the full map.
			Soldiers who cannot find a way stop as close as they can get. Any other order cancels the march.
			'''
	}

	Armory: {
		Title: The Armory
		Body:
			'''
			The Armory is a 1000-slot store with issue rows for weapons, armor sets, ammo and potions.
			Levies of any Barracks within 120 tiles arm themselves from it, best gear first.
			Right-click it to open it. The tabs along the top are Items, Rules, Vanity and Logistics.
			Each Logistical Center within 40 tiles adds 250 storage slots.
			An item a soldier carries shows in the grid as in use. Click it to take it back. Its owner gets the Armory's next pick.
			'''
	}

	BlockBreakers: {
		Title: Block-breaking weapons
		Body:
			'''
			Soldiers never break blocks or change the world until you allow it.
			Putting a weapon that breaks blocks, moves liquid or changes the biome into the Armory opens a warning with Confirm and Cancel.
			Cancel changes nothing. The weapon stays in storage, is never issued, and the warning returns when you deposit it again.
			Confirm allows that weapon in this world. Soldiers use it and break blocks the way you would. The choice is saved with the world and shared with every player.
			Picks, axes, hammers and drills are never offered.
			'''
	}

	ItemsPutIn: {
		Title: Items tab - putting things in
		Body:
			'''
			Click an item in your pack to pick it up, then click a storage slot to set it down. Shift-click an item in your pack to send the whole stack to storage. Favorited items and coins stay.
			Deposit junk moves everything levies can use that is not favorited into storage: weapons, throwables, armor, accessories, hooks, dyes, potions and ammo. Favorite what you want to keep.
			Loot all takes everything back out. Sort groups storage and packs it to the front.
			Search, the type button and the rarity swatches narrow what the grid shows.
			Hover an empty storage slot for a reminder of what levies use.
			'''
	}

	ItemsWeapons: {
		Title: Items tab - weapons and armor
		Body:
			'''
			Levies wield nearly any weapon a player can: swords, spears, guns, bows, magic, whips, boomerangs, yoyos, flails, summon staves, sentries, held weapons and thrown weapons.
			They charge charge weapons and switch a weapon's firing modes when the situation calls for it.
			Weapons in storage or on the weapon rack are issued best first. The rack is optional. Hover a weapon to see its class and rank.
			Armor goes in storage or the armor set slots. The armor set slots are optional: armor in them is used first.
			Levies prefer a complete matching set so the set bonus works.
			A levy wears armor of his weapon's class, such as a summoner's hood or a mage's cap, when the Armory has some no more than three tiers behind its best armor. The class comes from his weapon, so the Rules tab decides it too.
			'''
	}

	ItemsGear: {
		Title: Items tab - accessories, hooks and ammo
		Body:
			'''
			Accessories go in storage. Levies wear the best ones that suit their weapon and they work as they would on you. Wings take a slot.
			A pet or helper from a levy's accessory follows that levy and leaves when he falls.
			A Levy has 1 accessory slot, a Man-at-Arms 3 and a Champion 5.
			Grappling hooks go in storage. Each levy carries the best hook, swings across gaps and climbs cliffs with it.
			Ammo goes in the ammo row or storage. Ammo in the row is used first. Each levy carries one kind of ammo in a pouch and refills it from the Armory.
			'''
	}

	ItemsConsumables: {
		Title: Items tab - potions, throwables and sidearms
		Body:
			'''
			The first potion slot holds a healing or mana potion. Levies drink it when hurt, and casters sip mana potions when low.
			The other potion slots hold buff potions for the whole army. Buff potions are only used from these slots.
			Consumable throwables, such as grenades and throwing knives, go in storage. A levy refills a thrown stack from the Armory when it runs low.
			The last weapon slot is the sidearm. A minion staff or sentry there goes to a levy who is not a summoner, if he has no other sidearm.
			A levy in class armor carries a sidearm of his own damage class, such as a gun or bow for a ranger and a staff for a mage. This grows stricter as the world advances, and an off-class sidearm is taken only when it is far better. He never carries a second copy of his primary.
			Two more slots hold summon weapons: a sentry staff and a minion staff, and a summoner leads with a whip. A levy who is not a summoner carries at most one summon weapon, in a free sidearm slot. A sentry staff keeps one sentry up, plus one for each sentry slot the levy's gear gives.
			'''
	}

	Rules: {
		Title: Rules tab
		Body:
			'''
			The Rules tab decides which weapons the levies draw. Rules apply from the top, and the upper rule wins.
			Class balance sets how many levies carry each class. A limit gives way before a levy is left unarmed.
			Category rule prefers, orders, includes or requires a kind of weapon, such as bows or guns. Weight only tilts the pick. Order, Include and At least are firm, and outrank the armor a levy wears.
			Item list names items that are never used, or the only items used.
			Re-arm deals the whole army again at once. Without it, levies come to a changed rule on their own, a few at a time.
			Hover a weapon in storage to set its rank to Auto, Set, Never use or Prefer.
			'''
	}

	Vanity: {
		Title: Vanity tab
		Body:
			'''
			Vanity sets change how levies look, never what they can do.
			Press New set to add a row with head, body and legs vanity and a dye for each.
			A set is worn by all levies, by one class, or by chosen levies.
			For each slot the most specific row wins: named levies, then a class, then everyone.
			'''
	}

	LogisticsCraft: {
		Title: Logistics tab - orders and crafting
		Body:
			'''
			Orders craft sets and weapons into the Armory on their own.
			New starts an order. Add the item on your cursor, or + Set for the whole armor set of a piece.
			Orders craft at stations within 30 tiles. They can buy missing materials from shopkeepers, up to a budget.
			Pause or delete an order at any time. What it made stays in the Armory.
			Craft items browses every recipe and crafts at once from your pack, nearby chests and the Armory.
			'''
	}

	LogisticsTrade: {
		Title: Logistics tab - buy and sell
		Body:
			'''
			Buy lists what shopkeepers in reach sell. Click an item to buy it. A standing buy gets one item, the best gun or the best ammo each time an order runs, until its count or budget is met.
			Sell mass-sells Armory items to the best-paying shopkeeper in reach. Pick a kind and a tier and press Select matching, or drag over slots to select a block.
			Shimmer back returns the materials of items that shimmer apart.
			To storage sends the selection to Magic Storage, or to a chest beside the Armory.
			Clear out lists every weapon, armor piece and accessory below a tier, from the Armory, nearby chests and the Magic Storage network. Preview the list, then confirm to sell it, or to take it apart when a Decrafting Interface is linked. Favourited and preferred items stay.
			The Storage page lists what the army cannot use: blocks, tools, banners and vanity. Preview the list, then send it to Magic Storage, or to a chest beside the Armory. Weapons, armor, accessories, ammo, potions, coins and favourited items stay.
			'''
	}

	LogisticsFind: {
		Title: Logistics tab - find
		Body:
			'''
			Find lists the items in your Armory. Click one to look for it.
			A Seeker potion makes the chosen item glow in tiles and chests, with an arrow to the nearest.
			Stop finding clears the choice.
			'''
	}

	Reforge: {
		Title: Reforging
		Body:
			'''
			The Reforge button at the bottom of the Items tab opens the reforge panel beside the Armory.
			Choose the weapons: those in hand, recent additions, ones you pick by hand, or a whole class and type.
			Choose what to roll for: a floor from Good to Best, or one named modifier.
			Each roll costs the standard reforge price from the Barracks treasury.
			A spending cap stops the run. Items are never lost.
			'''
	}

	Heart: {
		Title: Armory Heart and Magic Storage
		Body:
			'''
			With Magic Storage installed, an Armory Heart joins the nearest Armory to a Magic Storage network.
			Connect the heart to a Storage Heart with connectors.
			Orders and buying draw from the network, and overflow goes into it.
			With the option "Magic Storage crafts with armory racks" on, the network's Crafting Interface crafts with the armory's racks: storage and the weapon rack. A weapon crafted from an armory weapon goes back to that armory. Taking an armory item out through the Crafting Interface (click, shift-click or right-click on the result) takes it off the armory. Favorited items and gear a levy carries are never used. Single player only.
			An Armory near a Storage Heart, Storage Access or Remote Access can issue gear from that network.
			Armories in a settlement with a pylon pool their stored gear with each other.
			'''
	}

	Officers: {
		Title: Officers
		Body:
			'''
			Officers are town NPCs conscripted into the army. They leave their post, march with the army and bring a special skill.
			Press Officers on the Barracks panel to commission townsfolk from every town on the pylon network.
			You start with 3 command slots. The Wall of Flesh, a mechanical boss, Plantera, Golem and Moon Lord each add one.
			A commission costs a fee once and a wage every dawn from the settlement's treasuries and stored coin.
			An unpaid officer goes off duty until paid. A fallen officer is gone until rehoused.
			Officers take your orders and stances with the soldiers: follow, hold, return, hunt, hold fire and spread out.
			'''
	}

	OfficersSupport: {
		Title: Officers - support
		Body:
			'''
			The Nurse heals the most wounded soldier within reach to full, paid from the treasury.
			The Guide makes soldiers near him aim truer and calls a volley. The Arms Dealer does the same and also stops them spending ammo.
			The Princess gives nearby soldiers Morale: more damage, defense and crit.
			The Angler keeps nearby soldiers Well Fed.
			The Dryad marks enemies so the army hits them harder, and gives nearby soldiers defense and regeneration.
			'''
	}

	OfficersUtility: {
		Title: Officers - utility
		Body:
			'''
			The Merchant adds coin to the treasury for every kill near him, and hands soldiers ammo and potions.
			The Tax Collector marks enemies and taxes their corpses into the treasury.
			The Goblin Tinkerer reforges soldiers' weapons on a timer, paid from the treasury.
			The Painter's paint cuts the defense and damage of enemies it soaks.
			The Demolitionist lobs grenades and dynamite. The Witch Doctor envenoms hits and calls voodoo spirits.
			'''
	}

	OfficersSummoners: {
		Title: Officers - summoners
		Body:
			'''
			The Pirate Captain conscripts flintlock gunners and a cannon.
			The Clothier raises skeletons. The Wizard enchants flying swords.
			The Zoologist calls a creature from the Bestiary.
			The Cyborg deploys combat drones, and ore and treasure glow around his commander.
			Fallen followers are replaced on a timer, and their damage grows as bosses fall.
			'''
	}

	Potions: {
		Title: Spectator and Seeker potions
		Body:
			'''
			Spectator lasts 20 minutes. Enemies ignore you and cannot hurt you, and your weapons, summons and building tools stop working. Lava, falls and drowning end it.
			You stay within 60 tiles of where you drank it. It cannot be drunk within 5 seconds of damage or while a boss you have not beaten is alive, and such a boss appearing ends it.
			Cancel it during a boss fight and you die. You also die if every soldier falls and no manpower is left in a boss fight.
			With A Guide to Autonomy, your levies act on their own while you watch: fearless, falling back to heal, and going for the biggest threat.
			Seeker lasts 12 minutes. Choose one item and tiles and chests that hold it glow, with an arrow to the nearest.
			'''
	}

	Provisions: {
		Title: Provisions
		Body:
			'''
			A meal feeds the nearest soldiers within 60 tiles: the Fish Fry Platter 10, the Fruit Basket 15, the Happy Burger Box 25 and the Grand Feast 50. It gives Well Fed for 30 minutes and Morale for 5.
			The Case of Fizz gives every soldier within 60 tiles low gravity and a double jump. Knockback hits them far harder.
			The Iceberg Tray gives every soldier within 60 tiles immunity to lava and fire, and lets them swim through lava. Their aim error grows.
			The Fizz Can and the Iceberg Shot are single drinks. The Armory can issue them from a buff slot.
			A provision is refused, and kept, when no soldier is in reach.
			'''
	}

	Cameras: {
		Title: Cameras
		Body:
			'''
			Every levy has a camera slot that only a GoPro fits. Put GoPros in the Armory and they are issued one per levy, and a levy that carries one can be watched.
			Use a GoPro to see through the next GoPro levy. Right-click it to open the camera view: pick a levy or camera, hide the HUD, or stop. Esc stops.
			Place a Static Camera, then right-click it from anywhere in reach to see its view. The mouse wheel zooms out.
			The Mind Link takes direct control of the levy nearest your cursor. Right-click lets go. If you die, you control a levy until you respawn.
			The Spectral Lens makes levies, their weapons and their shots see-through. Right-click it to switch between 85%, 95% and 99%. Only you see the effect.
			'''
	}

	Recorder: {
		Title: Fight recorder
		Body:
			'''
			The recorder saves each boss fight as a .levyrec file, and one recording of each event. Under Settings, Mod Configuration, Levy: Fight recorder choose Off, Boss fights or All fights.
			Type /levyrec start [name] to begin a recording, /levyrec stop to end it and print its path, /levyrec status to see it, and /levyrec folder to print the folder. A recording stops when the boss dies or after 10 minutes.
			Recordings go to Levy/Recordings in the tModLoader save folder unless you set another folder. A file stops at 200 MB and the oldest recordings are deleted past 2048 MB.
			When a boss fight ends, chat shows the result, your damage per second, what killed you and how the army held. The full report is saved as a text file next to the recording.
			The warning "The line is breaking!" appears when most of the army is shaken or falling fast in a boss fight.
			'''
	}

	OldOnesArmy: {
		Title: Old One's Army staves
		Body:
			'''
			The Levy Rod, Levy Cane and Levy Staff plant a levy sentry at your cursor during the Old One's Army.
			Each sentry costs 5 Etherian Mana and must be within 40 tiles of you. The Rod calls up 1 levy, the Cane 2 and the Staff 3.
			The Tavernkeep sells them for Defender Medals: 10, 25 and 50. The Cane is stocked after a mechanical boss and the Staff after Golem.
			He also sells the Barracks Keg, the Case of Stout and the Rum Cooler. Levies within 60 tiles cheer, then gain 8 defense, 15% damage or 10% critical chance for 5 minutes, and sway and aim 60% worse while drunk. Each costs 4 or 5 silver.
			'''
	}

	EventBooks: {
		Title: Event guide books
		Body:
			'''
			Five books drop from events at 1 in 50. Each is used up once per world and applies to the whole army.
			A Guide to Conscription, from the Blood Moon: each town NPC provides 15 manpower.
			A Guide to Drill, from the Goblin Army: militia aim 25% better. A Guide to Plunder, from pirates: hauled coins are worth 50% more.
			A Guide to Fortification, from the Old One's Army: soldiers within 60 tiles of a Barracks gain 4 defense.
			A Guide to Discipline, from the Solar Eclipse: shots are 35% tighter.
			'''
	}

	ProgressionGuides: {
		Title: Progression guide books
		Body:
			'''
			A Guide to Firepower drops from the Wall of Flesh: militia gain 100 health and 80 mana, and every Armory gains 2 buff slots.
			A Guide to Healthcare gives militia 150 health. A Guide to the Arcane Arts gives them 60 mana. Both are crafted at a Crystal Ball and a Bookcase.
			A Guide to Expertise drops from the Lunatic Cultist: every soldier gains 1 accessory slot.
			A Guide to Engineering unlocks combat drones at every Barracks. It needs the Mechanization mod.
			A Guide to Autonomy drops during Slime Rain and unlocks map orders.
			With Calamity, progression assistance adds more options: see the Calamity progression page.
			'''
	}

	ProgressionCalamity: {
		Title: Calamity progression assistance
		Body:
			'''
			The Superpowered Magnet re-forms the Exodium planetoids after Moon Lord, with a cooldown between uses. The Slime Agitator starts a slime rain that includes Calamity's ore slimes. It works in any weather and leaves an ordinary rain falling. It cannot be used while a slime rain is up or in a remix world.
			Extra recipes make Scoria Ore from Hellstone and Chlorophyte after Golem, and the Magnet and Agitator themselves.
			Calamity ore generation multiplies the ore a boss event makes from then on. Auric Ore regeneration makes Yharon generate it again on repeat kills.
			Jungle monsters can drop Uelibloom Ore after Providence.
			Providence and post-Providence material drop rates multiply Divine Geode, Unholy Essence and Uelibloom Ore. All of it is off by default and needs Calamity.
			'''
	}

	Settings: {
		Title: Settings and /levysetup
		Body:
			'''
			A setup popup opens in a new single player world. Type /levysetup, or press Setup on the Barracks panel, to open it again. Pick a preset. Default is the standard army. Relaxed gives more levies and bosses never target them. Hardcore gives fewer levies and bosses you have beaten target them.
			Customize sets how many levies you have at the start, by the Wall of Flesh, by Moon Lord and by the final boss, and how bosses treat them. New worlds start with 14 levies. Pre-hardmode growth follows how far along you are, so extra modded bosses do not slow it. Skip keeps the current settings. Multiplayer hosts use the mod config.
			Every other option is under Settings, Mod Configuration, Levy, with progression assistance on its own page: loot, Dungeon Spirits, Chlorophyte and Luminite.
			Machines stand down during boss fights is a Mechanization setting, off by default. While a boss lives, every drone and turret leaves the field and frees its NPC slot. Barracks drones return their weapons to the Armory. All of them come back when the boss is gone.
			Machines sleep when no player is near is a Mechanization setting. Turrets, placed drones and garrisons out of range pack away and free their NPC slots, then wake as they were. It is on by default. A boss that needs more NPC slots packs turrets and placed drones the same way until it is gone.
			With Wrath of the Gods loaded, /levyrift shows the state of its sky and /levyrift replay plays the covering of the sun again.
			'''
	}
}
